    animationPlayerDesc.doc = "Plays animation clips on skinned meshes.\012\012Parent it to the `Model` of a character, and it animates every skinned `MeshPart` under that model. A character is often several meshes that share a skeleton, such as a body, a shirt and trousers, and one clip moves all of them. Only one of the meshes needs to contain the animation; the clip is taken from the first one that has it, in tree order. Parented directly to a `MeshPart`, it animates only that mesh.\012\012Joints are matched between the meshes by name, not by index. A joint that a mesh does not have is skipped.\012\012Clips are sampled on simulation time, so the position of a clip at a given tick is the same in a replay as it was live. Clips play in place: there is no root motion, and the character's body moves the character.\012\012With a `Graph`, the player blends clips by itself. An animation graph defines which clips play in which state, how a walk blends into a run, when a jump starts and how long each fade lasts; the game only supplies values such as the character's speed. Tracks loaded from a script keep working alongside the graph. For reaching limbs and feet on uneven ground, add `IKControl` and `FootPlacement`.\012\012In a multiplayer match, the player replicates with its character, including `Graph` and `Retargeting`. Nothing is sent per tick: each machine runs its own copy of the graph from values that already replicate, such as the body's speed, whether it is on the ground, and its attributes.";
